<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaipėda Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Memel Revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> Revolt took place in January 1923, when Lithuanian irregulars, organised secretly by the Lithuanian government and presented as a local uprising, seized the Klaipėda Region from its French administration. The region had been detached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and placed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. The Allies accepted the result, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention">Klaipėda Convention</a> of 1924 made the region an autonomous part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaipeda-revolt.jpg" alt="Klaipėda Revolt" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original SVG file created by Matthead · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Prussia_1923-1939.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Klaipėda Region, north of the Neman River, was part of Lithuania Minor, with a large population of Lutheran Prussian Lithuanians and a port that was Lithuania's only viable access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> it was detached from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 10 January 1920 and placed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, and French troops under General Dominique Joseph Odry arrived in February; Gabriel Jean Petisné then headed the civilian administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> hoped to gain influence through a union with Lithuania, and after Lithuania refused a British offer in March 1922 to trade its claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> for the region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> favoured a free city like Danzig.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After a Prussian Lithuanian delegation failed before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a>, the Lithuanian government decided in secret on 20 November 1922 to stage a local revolt, on the model of Żeligowski's Mutiny. Prime Minister Ernestas Galvanauskas kept the plan even from the First Seimas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-riflemens-union">Lithuanian Riflemen's Union</a> under Vincas Krėvė-Mickevičius provided the men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> let it be known it would not interfere, and the Soviet Foreign Minister Georgy Chicherin offered support.</p><p>On 10 January 1923, 1,090 volunteers in civilian clothes crossed the border under Jonas Polovinskas, who had taken the name Jonas Budrys. A Supreme Committee for the Salvation of Lithuania Minor under Martynas Jankus claimed power, and Erdmonas Simonaitis formed a new Directorate. By 11 January the rebels held the region except the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a>, where fighting broke out on 15 January; 12 insurgents, two French soldiers and one German policeman were killed before a ceasefire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> protested and threatened force, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> supported the transfer, and the League, which was then dividing the neutral zone of the Vilnius Region, treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s compensation. On 17 February 1923 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a> transferred the region to Lithuania, on condition of autonomy, payment of Allied administration costs and an internationalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-convention">Klaipėda Convention</a>, prepared by a commission under the American Norman Davis, was hailed as a Lithuanian diplomatic victory, but it limited the central government's powers. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> exploited its weaknesses, and a German ultimatum took the region in March 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Klaipėda Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Klaipėda Revolt</b> of January 1923 was the last armed conflict in Lithuania before World War II; in 1938, 1939 and 1940 a weaker Lithuania accepted ultimatums rather than fight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1923 Lithuanian irregulars staged the <b>Klaipėda Revolt</b>, and the Allies accepted Lithuanian sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/state/klaipeda-region">Klaipėda Region</a></li></ul></section>
